<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Amazing Community – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 10px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1 of 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Amazing Community</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This School Has Been Here a Long Time!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Recognize the main ideas of the conversation when interacting in group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can determine the gist of simple stories about community traditions and events (e.g., 'In our community, we celebrate La Mejorana Festival with music and dancing.' 'The festival takes place in the town square every September.'). Can identify details about familiar stories about the community and its people (e.g., 'One landmark of the community is the old church.'). Can recognize main points in group discussions about the community (e.g., 'We have planned a community celebration for this weekend.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or projects images of a school, a park, a market, and different community members (e.g., a teacher, a farmer, a police officer, a musician, a carpenter, a gardener). Students identify them by name. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" "Who is this person?" "Where do they work?" to elicit vocabulary like 'school', 'park', 'market', 'teacher', 'farmer'. The teacher then asks, "Do you think this school has been here a long time?" connecting to the theme.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to the images and verbally introduces new vocabulary (e.g., 'community', 'neighborhood', 'festival', 'event', 'traditions', 'job', 'volunteer', 'share', 'celebrate'). The teacher clearly pronounces each word, emphasizing stress in compound nouns like 'neighborhood' (stress on 'neigh-'). The teacher also models short and long vowel sounds, for example, saying 'cook' (/kʊk/) and 'school' (/skuːl/), asking students to repeat and notice the difference. The teacher says, "Listen carefully: 'cook' - short /ʊ/, 'school' - long /uː'."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'festival' a sad or a happy event?" (Target: 'event', 'festival') - Expected answer: "Happy!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I 'volunteer', do I get paid?" (Target: 'volunteer', 'job') - Expected answer: "No!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is your 'neighborhood' a small place or a big area with houses and streets?" (Target: 'neighborhood') - Expected answer: "A big area with houses and streets."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Amazing Community"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue (approx. 2 minutes) between two community members, an elderly 'teacher' and a 'gardener', discussing the history of their 'school' and an upcoming 'community festival'. The dialogue includes simple sentences in present simple and past simple (e.g., 'This school has been here for 50 years.' 'Last year, we celebrated the festival in the park.'), and touches upon community roles and activities. It uses vocabulary: 'school', 'community', 'teacher', 'gardener', 'festival', 'park', 'celebrate', 'before', 'last year', 'every year'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can understand simple direct information in short, clear, and slow speech on familiar topics, such as community history and events, when supported by visual cues.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listen (Gist):</b> Students listen to the dialogue once. The teacher asks broad questions: "Who is talking?" (e.g., a teacher and a gardener), "What are they talking about?" (e.g., the school and a festival).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listen (Details):</b> Students listen again. They complete a True/False exercise based on specific statements about the audio (e.g., "The school is new." - False, "They will have a festival next month." - True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third Listen (Identifying Key Info):</b> Students listen one more time and match phrases or details to the correct speaker or event (e.g., match 'talks about old school' to 'teacher', 'works in the garden' to 'gardener').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for specific vocabulary ('community', 'school', 'festival', 'teacher', 'farmer', 'gather', 'celebrate', 'visit') and grammar structures (Present Simple and Past Simple with time expressions 'every year', 'last year', 'before', 'after'; Present Perfect 'has been'). Pragmatic function: 'Share community activities: Our community has a festival each year.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening Cloze):</b> The teacher plays segments of the audio dialogue again. Students have a handout with sentences from the dialogue, but with target vocabulary words or time expressions missing (e.g., "The community _____ at the park _____ the festival _____ year."). Students listen and fill in the blanks. (Expected answers: 'gathers', 'before', 'every'). This reinforces specific listening for words and phrases.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Grammar Identification):</b> The teacher plays 5-7 short sentences extracted from the dialogue (or similar sentences about the community). For each sentence, students identify whether it describes an action in the 'present' (Present Simple), 'past' (Past Simple), or a continuous experience up to now (Present Perfect). For example, after hearing "Last year, we had a big celebration," students write "Past." After "Our teacher has worked here for ten years," students write "Present Perfect." This builds awareness of grammatical forms through listening.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students compare their answers from the cloze activity and grammar identification. They can replay specific parts of the audio on their devices (if available) or ask the teacher to replay for clarification. This encourages active listening and collaborative problem-solving. The teacher circulates, listens to student discussions, and provides feedback, clarifying pronunciation of 'cook' and 'school' if needed.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Amazing Community"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion after Listening. Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher plays a new short audio clip (approx. 1 minute) which is an announcement about a historical community event or a new community initiative related to the school. (e.g., "Our community has planned a celebration for the school's 60th anniversary next month. Volunteers are needed. What does your neighbor do?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the audio twice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After listening, in their groups, students discuss and collaboratively answer three specific questions provided by the teacher on a worksheet:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is the main event discussed in the announcement?</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When will it happen? (e.g., 'next month')</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Who is needed to help? (e.g., 'volunteers')</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student from each group shares their answers with the class. This stage emphasizes recognizing main points in group discussions about the community and sharing community activities.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during Stage 4 group discussions, focusing on students' ability to identify main ideas and details from the listening input and contribute to group understanding. (e.g., "Student recognizes main event," "Student identifies key details," "Student collaborates effectively.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students listen to a final, very short audio clip (e.g., a community member briefly describing their job as a 'vendor' at the 'market' or a local 'tradition' like "Our community celebrates with delicious food and music."). After listening, students individually write down 2-3 main ideas or key details they understood from the clip on an exit slip.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies the main idea of the short audio clip (e.g., "The person is a vendor at the market.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes at least two key details (e.g., "sells fresh fruits," "works every day").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates active listening skills (e.g., focused attention, ability to recall information).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on their exit slip:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I listened well to learn about __________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I heard and understood was __________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you find easy about listening to conversations about our community today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging when trying to recognize the main ideas and details?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in terms of the listening activities? Were students able to recognize main ideas and details effectively?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next listening lesson to further support students' comprehension of spoken English related to community themes? (e.g., longer pauses, more repetition, visual support).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Today we practiced listening to stories and information about our community and school. Next lesson, we will read a short text with similar words and ideas about community traditions and events to build our reading comprehension skills."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>